    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe wiring and the per-step function design are idiomatic \u2014 `FlagBulkSignup` is a correctly-sized single-responsibility step slotted into the POST pipeline, and persisting the derived flag as an `Owner` column (schema across all four dialects + MapStruct mapping + `readOnly` OpenAPI field) faithfully mirrors the existing `namesakeCount` pattern, so that part reads as natural for this codebase.\n\nOne structural issue:\n\n- **Duplicated \"owners sharing this registration date\" count between `FlagBulkSignup` and `RejectOverDailyLimit`.** The two classes contain the *identical* computation \u2014\n  ```java\n  LocalDate registrationDate = owner.getRegistrationDate();\n  long count = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n          .filter(existing -> registrationDate.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate()))\n          .count();\n  ```\n  differing only in the threshold constant and the terminal action (throw vs. set a flag). The new Javadoc even calls this out (\"the same accumulation path as `RejectOverDailyLimit`, at a lower threshold\"). This is distinct from `CountOwnerNamesakes`, which scans on a genuinely different predicate (name match), so that step is legitimately its own thing \u2014 but these two now carry the same daily-count logic verbatim.\n\n  Since `rejectOverDailyLimit` already runs earlier in the pipeline (step before `assignHousehold`) and `flagBulkSignup` runs just before `save`, the daily count is a natural candidate to compute once and share rather than copy: have the reject step publish the count (`Out<Long>`, e.g. a `@Qualifier`-tagged `DailyOwnerCount`) and have `FlagBulkSignup` consume it via `@Val`, applying only the `> 80` comparison. That removes the duplicated predicate (and the second full-table scan), keeps each threshold rule in its own step, and leaves a single home for \"how many owners were created today.\"\n\nIf you prefer to keep the two steps fully independent (consistent with the codebase's one-scan-per-function habit), at minimum factor the shared filter into one small helper so the predicate isn't maintained in two places.",
